target: internal/record/record.go
verify: go test ./internal/record/ -count=1 -run 'TestConvergenceRules'
expect: a_cycle_with_no_agents_field_spends_nothing
why:    a cycle row with NO `agents` key spends nothing, which is the Python's `or 0`. Absent from ZERO of the real store's cycle rows (63 of them, measured 2026-10-09 two ways; the store is append-only and live, so the count drifts and the date is the point), so this pins the parity answer for a shape no writer produces rather than guarding live data — and `cmd_cycle`/the loop's step j2 always write the key, which argparse ENFORCES: runlog.py declares `--agents` as `type=int, required=True` on the cycle subparser, so a cycle row without it cannot be written. (An earlier version of this line credited `cmd_plan`, which writes the PLAN row and has no `agents` key at all — it writes `agent_cap` — and said "nothing enforces that", dropping a correct enforcement fact the version before it had stated.) An earlier version of this line claimed "17 of the real store's cycle rows omit it", which is the store's NUDGE-row count: the right store, the wrong question, and flattering, because it made the entry look like it guarded real data. The text it replaced ("absent from all 59 real cycle rows") was correct. Now anchored on AgentsSpent, the ONE place the rule lives: before the extraction it was written out three times (Convergence, Disclosure, push.Fingerprint) and this entry pinned only one of them, so the other two could have been edited without the catalog noticing — which is the duplication the extraction removed.
--- old
		if c.Agents != nil {
			n += *c.Agents
		}
--- new
		if c.Agents == nil {
			n++
		} else {
			n += *c.Agents
		}
